<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shamash-shum-ukin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 668–648 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shamash-shum-ukin was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from 668 to 648 BCE, a son of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>, who made him ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> under his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. In 652 BCE he rebelled with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldeans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. Ashurbanipal besieged Babylon, and Shamash-shum-ukin died when the city fell in 648 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin.jpg" alt="Shamash-shum-ukin" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Shamash-shum-ukin_BM_77611.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Šamaš-šuma-ukin was a son of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> and the elder brother of Esarhaddon's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. He was probably the second eldest son, younger only than the crown prince Sin-nadin-apli. When Sin-nadin-apli died unexpectedly in 674 BCE, Esarhaddon drew up new succession plans to avoid a crisis after his own death. Ashurbanipal, the fourth eldest son, was to inherit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, while Šamaš-šuma-ukin was designated heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, the two being called equal brothers. He was nevertheless to swear an oath of allegiance to Ashurbanipal. Why the elder son was bypassed for Assyria is not known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> at once on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>'s death, and Šamaš-šuma-ukin was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the spring of the next year. At his coronation the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>, taken from the city by his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> twenty years before, was returned to Babylon. Although he was ethnically and culturally Assyrian, he assimilated well: his inscriptions use Babylonian imagery to an extent not found before, he took part in the Babylonian New Year's festival, rebuilt the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, restored shrines and increased the land of the Ishtar temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>.</p><p>He remained a closely monitored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Contemporary records show him in control only of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and its vicinity, and governors of cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> as their king. When the Elamite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teumman">Teumman</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in 653 BCE he had to rely on Ashurbanipal for troops. Resentment grew, and in 652 BCE he revolted, seeking independence for Babylonia rather than the Assyrian throne. His allies included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldeans</a>, Arameans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, and perhaps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. For two years battles were fought across Babylonia, with several minor players repeatedly changing sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> were defeated near Der, and by 650 BCE Ashurbanipal's forces had besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a>, Kutha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Babylon fell in 648 BCE after a siege of two years, with famine in the city, and was plundered. Ashurbanipal's inscriptions describe a bloodbath. Šamaš-šuma-ukin's fate is unclear: historians traditionally hold that he burned himself in his palace, but contemporary texts say only that he met a cruel death and that the gods consigned him to a fire. Ashurbanipal then placed his official Kandalanu on the Babylonian throne as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>Assyrian scribes found the episode hard to record, since Šamaš-šuma-ukin was a legitimately installed king and a member of the royal family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s inscriptions often call him only the king, and later Assyrian kings do not mention him. Steles he had erected were mutilated, erasing his face, which points to a large-scale damnatio memoriae.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Šamaš-šuma-ukin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The civil war of 652–648 BCE between Ashurbanipal and his brother <b>Shamash-shum-ukin</b>, king of Babylon, had already shown how fragile Assyrian control was in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Bit-Amukani later joined revolts against Assyria, including that of <b>Shamash-shum-ukin</b>, and by the mid-7th century it vanished into Babylonian society.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-amukani">Bit-Amukani</a></li><li><blockquote>Merodach-baladan's grandson Nabu-bel-shumati joined <b>Shamash-shum-ukin's</b> revolt and died in Elam around 648 BCE, after which Bit-Yakin disappears as a power.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-yakin">Bit-Yakin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-658" aria-label="Babylon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5690 981 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Shamash-shum-ukin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6140 1251h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.52,44.42&amp;year=-658">What was here in 658 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
